course: dictionary
lesson: 5692
title: Two meanings of locomotive
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Locomotive is a word with two jobs. Most of the time it is a noun, a thing: a railway engine that pulls a train. «A freight train is a long line of wagons pulled by a strong locomotive.» The clue is «pulled by»: the locomotive is the engine at the front doing the pulling.
explain: «That is why one locomotive, the engine that pulls the train, can pull a whole line of heavy wagons, and also why a fast train needs a very long way to stop.» The sentence explains the word for you: the engine that pulls the train. When a text explains a hard word inside the sentence, use that explanation.
explain: In science, locomotive is an adjective, a describing word, and it is formal. It means to do with moving from place to place. Its noun cousin is locomotion, which is how animals move, such as walking, swimming or flying. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: Locomotive (noun) means a railway engine that pulls a train.
fact: Locomotive (adjective, science, formal) means to do with moving from place to place.
fact: Locomotion means how animals or things move from place to place, such as walking, swimming or flying.
fact: A freight train carries things like coal, containers, cars or stone, and one freight train can take dozens of lorries off the road.
fact: George Stephenson's «Rocket» (1829) was a famous steam locomotive. Other kinds include diesel and electric locomotives.
exercise: «A freight train is a long line of wagons pulled by a strong locomotive.» What does locomotive mean here? | A heavy load of stone | A station where trains wait | *A railway engine that pulls a train | A long line of wagons carrying coal
exercise: In science, a text says «locomotion» is how animals move. Which word means how animals move from place to place? | stone | container | *locomotion | wagon
exercise: Which of these is a locomotive, the thing that pulls a train? | A wagon full of coal | *A steam engine that pulls a train | A long line of cars | A container on a lorry
test: In science, which word means to do with moving from place to place? | Ancient | *Locomotive | Curt | Glum
test: «The Rocket», built in 1829, was a famous steam locomotive. What kind of word is locomotive here? | A verb, a doing word | *A noun, a thing: a railway engine | An adverb, a word for how | A pronoun, like he or she
exam: «A diesel locomotive pulls a long freight train.» Which word names the thing doing the pulling? | Wagon | Container | Freight | *Locomotive
